  "summary": "Masked Macho Ratón outwits Spanish power with laughter — Nicaragua's UNESCO drama.",
  "description": "El Güegüense (\"the wise old man\"), performed in Diriamba every January, is a satirical dance-drama of Nahuatl-Spanish origins where the masked indigenous elder Güegüense out-talks, dodges and mocks Spanish colonial officials and their taxes. Its masks are the visual heart: the Güegüense's creased sun-wizened face, the heavy-jawed Don Forcopío Pedrañes, and the caballito (little horse) ridden by a masked body. Performers dance between dialogue bursts to marimba and drums; UNESCO inscribed the work in 2005 as a Nicaraguan intangible masterpiece, one of Latin America's few UNESCO theater listings, and workshops keep carving cedar masks in the old patina style.",
  "culturalContext": "El Güegüense — UNESCO's 2005 Masterpiece — is Nicaragua's founding satire: the masked elder outwitting colonial authority performed in Diriamba since the 1600s, its cedar masks carved by family lineages, and its laughter-coded resistance read as the Nicaraguan national character in miniature.",
